          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get chest discomfort only when I am anxious, never on exertion. How do I tell the difference, and what does guidance describe about assessing it?",
          "answer": "The rule check found no emergency pattern in what you described, but that is not a clearance. A symptom the check does not list can still be serious, so get medical advice promptly rather than waiting. If your chest discomfort is new, getting worse, or unexplained, see a clinician soon.\n\nFeeling anxious does not settle the question. The coronary artery disease note says stable angina can be brought on by \"exertion or emotional stress,\" so stress alone does not rule out a heart cause. The anxiety note says a first panic attack with chest pain is treated as a physical emergency until a heart and breathing cause has been excluded. It also says the two \"are not distinguishable by how they feel,\" and that a person with an anxiety diagnosis can still have a cardiac event. The reflux note says reflux should not be assumed to be the cause either.\n\n**Call emergency services now** if chest discomfort comes with any of these:\n- Pressure, tightness, squeezing, heaviness or burning in the centre or left of the chest that lasts more than a few minutes, or that goes away and comes back.\n- Spread to the arms, shoulders, neck, jaw, back or upper abdomen.\n- Breathlessness, cold sweat, nausea or vomiting, light-headedness, or a sense of impending doom.\n- Discomfort at rest, or brought on by less effort than usual.\n- Any chest discomfort if you have known heart disease, diabetes, or several cardiovascular risk factors.\n\nDo not assume an episode is \"just anxiety\" because it happened during an anxious moment. Cardiac pain can also feel atypical, especially in women, older adults and people with diabetes, and missing classic pain is not reassuring. If you have any of the features above, do not wait for an appointment.\n\nThe sources do not describe a specific method for telling anxiety-related chest pain apart from heart pain, and they do not say when that pattern is safe to attribute to anxiety. I could not complete a further search, so this is based only on the passages I had. A clinician can review your history and risk factors and decide whether tests such as an ECG are needed. To help, write down when episodes happen, how long they last, what helps, and what you feel. The sources I had do not grade how strong the evidence is for these points, and they showed no disagreement between authorities on this topic.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
